# hk as a CONFIG-BASED git hook (git 2.54+), registered once for the machine.
#
# `hook.<friendly-name>.command` + `.event` is git's own multi-hook mechanism:
# unlike core.hooksPath it does not replace anything, and traditional
# .git/hooks/* still run alongside it. So this coexists with the
# init.templateDir hooks — git-spice's post-checkout and the betterleaks
# pre-commit floor — instead of displacing them. See git/CLAUDE.md.
#
# What it buys: any repo with an hk.pkl gets its hooks with NO per-clone
# install, and a repo without one is a silent no-op, so this is safe to have on
# globally. `mise run hooks` (per-clone `hk install`) remains for a clone made
# with an older git or a machine whose user gitconfig is not ours.
#
# The friendly-name must NOT be a hook event name: `[hook "pre-commit"]` is a
# fatal ambiguity with hook.<event>.enabled and makes every git command fail.
# Hence hk-pre-commit / hk-commit-msg.
#
# Escape hatch: HK=0 git commit ...   (git commit --no-verify skips all hooks.)
#
# Deliberately hand-written rather than generated by `hk install --global`,
# which writes an absolute version-pinned mise install path
# (~/.local/share/mise/installs/hk/<version>/hk) into ~/.gitconfig — a symlink
# into this repo, so that path would be committed, break on the next
# `mise upgrade`, and be wrong on every other machine. The wrapper resolves hk
# at run time; see git/.local/bin/hk-git-hook.
[hook "hk-pre-commit"]
	command = ~/.local/bin/hk-git-hook pre-commit --staged
	event = pre-commit

[hook "hk-commit-msg"]
	command = ~/.local/bin/hk-git-hook commit-msg
	event = commit-msg

# pre-push is registered even though this repo's hk.pkl has no pre-push hook:
# other repos do (vol and vault-mobile run their full lint gate there), and a
# repo whose hk.pkl lacks the hook is a no-op like any other.
[hook "hk-pre-push"]
	command = ~/.local/bin/hk-git-hook pre-push
	event = pre-push
